# Fincai — AI Collaboration Rules
#
# These are the rules that governed AI-assisted ("vibe-coded") development of
# this project. They are real, enforced conventions — several are backed by CI
# tests in this repo, so breaking them fails the build.

## Compliance & public copy (CI-enforced)

- Never hardcode a disclaimer or risk disclosure inline. All disclosure copy is
  canonical in `shared/disclosures.ts` and must be imported from there.
- All public-facing copy (landing page, README.md, PRD.md, app bundle) is
  scanned by the compliance lint in
  `client/src/test/compliance-ci-checks.test.ts`. It hard-fails on:
  - superlative security/quality grades and guaranteed-outcome marketing,
  - any numeric performance figure that a human has not explicitly reviewed
    and allowlisted,
  - blanket confirmation claims that ignore the autonomous runner.
- Scope confirmation language to MANUAL trades only. An armed live strategy
  places and closes orders within the user's configured limits WITHOUT a
  separate confirmation for each order — copy must say so, in exactly those
  terms.
- No advice-like language. Fincai is software, not an investment adviser;
  never write copy that recommends buying or selling anything.

## Honesty rules

- Never fabricate market data. If a provider fails, surface an explicit
  "unavailable" state (HTTP 503 / empty portfolio), never an invented number.
  The only simulated data allowed is the clearly-labeled demo preview and
  paper runs.
- Never document a behavior the code does not implement. Verify every factual
  claim (provider order, middleware coverage, guard semantics) against the
  actual code path before writing it into README/PRD/marketing copy.
- Screenshots and demos illustrate software behavior, not trading results.

## Safety-first defaults (anything touching a brokerage)

- Paper (simulated) mode is the default everywhere; live trading is a
  separate, explicit per-run opt-in. Anonymous users are paper-only.
- Fail closed: position caps are centralized and re-checked against the live
  price at order entry; live entries/exits gate on market hours; the live
  entry path guards on direction (long-only V1).
- An order with an unknown outcome (failed call, restart mid-flight) pauses
  the run for human review. Never auto-retry an order that may have executed.
- Order-placing state transitions must be claimed atomically (compare-and-set)
  so a background tick and a manual stop cannot double-act on one run.
- OAuth callback URLs derive from trusted server configuration only — never
  from request Host headers. Broker tokens are stored AES-256-GCM encrypted;
  never log or persist plaintext credentials. State-changing routes validate
  CSRF (double-submit cookie/header).

## Architecture conventions

- TypeScript end-to-end. Shared types and the database schema live in
  `shared/schema.ts`; data access goes through the storage layer
  (`server/storage.ts`), not ad-hoc queries in routes.
- Route modules live under `server/routes/`; long-running engines (strategy
  runner, MCP session manager) are server-side singletons with explicit state
  machines persisted to the database.
- UI: shadcn/ui + Tailwind, lucide icons (no emojis in product UI), TanStack
  Query for server state, wouter for routing.
- Broker MCP tool schemas are discovered at runtime and are opaque at build
  time; order payloads keep redundant descriptor fields for compatibility.

## Workflow

- `npm run check` (TypeScript) and `npx vitest run` (test suite, including the
  compliance lint) must pass before any change ships.
- Docs are part of the product: README.md and PRD.md sit inside the compliance
  scan set, so copy edits run through the same gate as code.
